import CacCalculator from '../../components/CacCalculator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Customer Acquisition Cost (CAC) Calculator" description="Calculate CAC from your total marketing and sales spend divided by new customers acquired.">
  <CacCalculator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>How to Calculate Customer Acquisition Cost</h2>
  <p><strong>CAC = Total Sales &amp; Marketing Spend ÷ Number of New Customers Acquired</strong>, both measured over the same time period.</p>

  <h2>Why CAC alone doesn't tell the full story</h2>
  <p>CAC tells you what it costs to acquire a customer, but it's only meaningful next to customer lifetime value (LTV) — a low CAC with an even lower LTV is still a losing business, while a higher CAC can be perfectly healthy if LTV comfortably exceeds it.</p>

  <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter your total sales and marketing spend for a period.</li>
    <li>Enter the number of new customers acquired in that same period.</li>
    <li>See your CAC for that period.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>Spending $10,000 on sales and marketing in a month that brings in 50 new customers gives a CAC of $200 per customer.</p>

  <h2>Common CAC Calculation Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Excluding sales team salaries or tooling costs</strong> from the calculation, understating true CAC.</li>
    <li><strong>Mismatching the time period</strong> between spend and customers acquired — marketing spend today often produces customers weeks or months later, not in the same period.</li>
    <li><strong>Looking at CAC in isolation</strong> without comparing it to LTV, which is the number that actually determines whether the acquisition spend is profitable.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This CAC Calculator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Evaluating whether a marketing channel is worth continued investment</li>
    <li>Reporting acquisition efficiency to investors or leadership</li>
    <li>Comparing CAC trends month-over-month as spend scales</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Customer Acquisition Cost FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What's a "good" CAC?</strong> It depends entirely on your LTV and margins — a common rule of thumb is an LTV:CAC ratio of at least 3:1, but healthy ratios vary significantly by industry and business model.</p>
  <p><strong>How do you calculate cost per customer acquisition?</strong> Add up all sales and marketing spend for a period, then divide by the number of new customers acquired in that same period — the calculator above does this instantly.</p>
</ToolLayout>
